  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nRain showers return today and some could be moderate to heavy at\ntimes this afternoon and evening. A few thunderstorms are not\nout of the question later this afternoon and overnight with a\ncold front crossing the area. Forcing from the front could lead\nto thunderstorms, but minimal CAPE will keep the activity\nisolated. Severe weather is not expected.\n\nRainfall amounts will generally be between 0.50\" and 1.00\" with\nthe heaviest falling across southeast Ohio, northeast Kentucky, \nwestern West Virginia and portions of the mountains. Expecting \nsome pockets over an inch across our western most counties. \nThere still remains a 10% chance that some areas in southeast \nOhio, northeast Kentucky, and western West Virginia could get 2 \ninches. \n\nHowever, due to our recent dry spell, flooding is not expected,\noutside of localized drainage issues from nuisance leaf litter\nclogs. Nonetheless, there is a level 1/4 marginal risk for \nexcessive rainfall across our northeast Kentucky counties, \nportions of southeast Ohio, and the Tri-State Area of West \nVirginia. These are the areas where higher amounts of rainfall \nare most likely to occur. \n\nA secondary cold front crosses Friday into Saturday leading to\nchances for more showers across the area, especially across the\nnorthern lowlands and northeastern mountains due to upslope.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMuch cooler, slightly below to around seasonable temperatures\narrive behind the front Friday night and will linger into next \nweek. Our 70s and 60s for high temperatures this week will be\nreplaces with 50s and 40s come Saturday. Starting Friday night, \nlow temperatures in the upper 20s and 30s will be common area \nwide each night well into next week. Subsequently, multiple\nnights of frost/freeze concerns are in store this weekend across\nour active growing season counties.",
